<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Turns WUH010 on for the two projects that compile the package's own PRODUCTION sources:
  WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers.TypeCheck (Runtime/) and WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers.EditorCheck
  (Runtime/ + Editor/).

  TestCheck AND EditorTestCheck DELIBERATELY DO NOT REFERENCE THIS FILE. Do not "finish the job" by
  adding it to them. MEASURED with all four wired up: 346 sites, of which 286 are under Tests/ and
  are dominated by fixtures whose SUBJECT is the indexer: 54 in DictionaryExtensionTests, 31 in
  SerializableDictionaryTests, 16 in SerializableDictionaryJsonTests, 13 in
  SerializableSortedDictionaryTests, 6 in IntMapTests, and on down. `Assert.AreEqual(1, map["a"])`
  in a dictionary's own test is not a footgun; rewriting it through TryGetValue deletes the thing
  the test asserts. Exempting the test trees by PROJECT is the honest version of that, and it
  costs 286 pragmas fewer than the alternative. The 60 production sites are real and are swept separately.

  WUH010 (a dictionary read through the key indexer, where TryGetValue reports the miss) is the one
  member of the WUH### family that ships off by default: a read of a key that is known present is
  correct and ubiquitous, so on by default it would bury the other nine on a consumer's first build
  after a package upgrade. The package still holds itself to it, and this file is how.

  WHY A RULESET AND NOT AN .editorconfig. `dotnet_diagnostic.WUH010.severity` is matched against the
  SOURCE FILE's path. TypeCheck, EditorCheck, TestCheck and EditorTestCheck compile files out of
  Runtime/, Editor/ and Tests/ - none of which live under Generator~ - so a Generator~/.editorconfig
  would not reach a single analyzed file, and a repository-root one would also be read by Unity's
  own compilation of those same trees, which is the thing this must not change. A ruleset is
  attached to the PROJECT, which is also how the two test-tree gates above stay out of it, and it
  covers nothing a consumer builds.

  Generator~/Directory.Build.props makes warnings errors, so a WUH010 finding fails those two builds.
  That is the point: it is the package's own opt-in, made binding.

  Nothing else is listed here. A rule a ruleset does not name keeps its own default, so the other
  nine WUH### rules and every WPROTO### rule are unaffected.
-->
<RuleSet Name="Unity Helpers production check projects" Description="Package-internal analyzer opt-ins for the Generator~ gates over Runtime/ and Editor/." ToolsVersion="15.0">
  <Rules AnalyzerId="WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers.Analyzers" RuleNamespace="WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers.Analyzers">
    <Rule Id="WUH010" Action="Warning" />
  </Rules>
</RuleSet>
